WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.140
How does Prim's algorithm work?

2
00:00:02.680 --> 00:00:04.200
Imagine eight places that need

3
00:00:04.200 --> 00:00:05.400
to be connected by cables.

4
00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:07.440
The numbers are cable costs;

5
00:00:07.940 --> 00:00:08.980
we want to connect every

6
00:00:08.980 --> 00:00:10.340
place for the smallest total

7
00:00:10.340 --> 00:00:11.680
cost, without making a loop.

8
00:00:12.140 --> 00:00:14.060
A minimum spanning tree connects

9
00:00:14.060 --> 00:00:15.940
all eight places using seven

10
00:00:15.940 --> 00:00:18.040
edges. It minimises their total

11
00:00:18.040 --> 00:00:19.920
cost, not the distance from

12
00:00:19.920 --> 00:00:22.040
one starting place. Green will

13
00:00:22.040 --> 00:00:23.020
show the tree we have

14
00:00:23.020 --> 00:00:23.820
already built.

15
00:00:24.260 --> 00:00:26.000
Start at any place; we

16
00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:27.600
will start at A. Blue

17
00:00:27.600 --> 00:00:29.500
edges are possible next steps:

18
00:00:29.920 --> 00:00:31.320
one endpoint is green and

19
00:00:31.320 --> 00:00:33.160
the other is outside. Choose

20
00:00:33.160 --> 00:00:34.960
the cheapest blue edge, add

21
00:00:34.960 --> 00:00:36.760
its outside endpoint, and repeat.

22
00:00:37.420 --> 00:00:39.360
From A, the choices cost

23
00:00:39.360 --> 00:00:42.200
two, three, and three. Add

24
00:00:42.200 --> 00:00:43.520
the edge from A to

25
00:00:43.520 --> 00:00:45.520
B, costing two. The edge

26
00:00:45.520 --> 00:00:46.760
from C to E costs

27
00:00:46.760 --> 00:00:48.960
only one, but neither endpoint

28
00:00:48.960 --> 00:00:50.240
is in our tree yet,

29
00:00:50.560 --> 00:00:51.720
so we cannot use it

30
00:00:51.720 --> 00:00:52.000
now.

31
00:00:52.580 --> 00:00:54.440
Now look outwards from both

32
00:00:54.440 --> 00:00:56.440
A and B, not just

33
00:00:56.440 --> 00:00:58.120
from B. Three possible edges

34
00:00:58.120 --> 00:01:00.520
tie at cost three; choose

35
00:01:00.520 --> 00:01:01.760
the edge from A to

36
00:01:01.760 --> 00:01:04.020
C. This adds C while

37
00:01:04.020 --> 00:01:05.520
keeping one connected tree.

38
00:01:05.900 --> 00:01:08.320
Adding C exposes the edge

39
00:01:08.320 --> 00:01:10.220
from C to E, costing

40
00:01:10.220 --> 00:01:12.480
one. Add that edge now.

41
00:01:13.160 --> 00:01:14.280
A new choice can be

42
00:01:14.280 --> 00:01:15.880
cheaper than the previous choice;

43
00:01:16.640 --> 00:01:17.940
Prim does not take all

44
00:01:17.940 --> 00:01:19.600
graph edges in sorted order.

45
00:01:20.180 --> 00:01:21.600
The edge from B to

46
00:01:21.600 --> 00:01:23.520
E costs three, but both

47
00:01:23.520 --> 00:01:25.660
endpoints are already green. Adding

48
00:01:25.660 --> 00:01:27.100
it would close the highlighted

49
00:01:27.100 --> 00:01:28.640
loop through B, A, C,

50
00:01:28.640 --> 00:01:30.420
and E. Ignore it: the

51
00:01:30.420 --> 00:01:31.640
next edge must bring in

52
00:01:31.640 --> 00:01:32.240
a new place.

53
00:01:32.780 --> 00:01:34.900
The cheapest eligible edge now

54
00:01:34.900 --> 00:01:36.560
connects A to D for

55
00:01:36.560 --> 00:01:38.720
three. Add D to the

56
00:01:38.720 --> 00:01:40.840
tree. We compare all blue

57
00:01:40.840 --> 00:01:42.880
edges again after every addition.

58
00:01:43.780 --> 00:01:45.560
To leave our current tree,

59
00:01:46.060 --> 00:01:47.620
the costs are four, six,

60
00:01:47.780 --> 00:01:49.980
seven, and eight. Choose the

61
00:01:49.980 --> 00:01:51.240
edge from E to H,

62
00:01:51.460 --> 00:01:53.080
costing four. The cheaper edge

63
00:01:53.080 --> 00:01:54.180
from G to H was

64
00:01:54.180 --> 00:01:55.860
not eligible before this step,

65
00:01:56.280 --> 00:01:58.200
because both places were outside.

66
00:01:58.780 --> 00:02:01.160
Now H is inside, so

67
00:02:01.160 --> 00:02:02.300
the edge from H to

68
00:02:02.300 --> 00:02:04.180
G becomes a valid choice.

69
00:02:04.640 --> 00:02:06.420
Add G for two, which

70
00:02:06.420 --> 00:02:07.620
is cheaper than the other

71
00:02:07.620 --> 00:02:09.920
crossing edges. The blue boundary

72
00:02:09.920 --> 00:02:11.540
changes whenever the green tree

73
00:02:11.540 --> 00:02:11.960
grows.

74
00:02:12.500 --> 00:02:14.480
Only F is still outside.

75
00:02:15.160 --> 00:02:17.160
Its possible connections cost five,

76
00:02:17.520 --> 00:02:20.340
six, seven, eight, and nine.

77
00:02:20.860 --> 00:02:22.280
Choose the edge from H

78
00:02:22.280 --> 00:02:23.980
to F for five; now

79
00:02:23.980 --> 00:02:25.300
all eight places are connected.

80
00:02:25.740 --> 00:02:27.840
Our selected costs are two,

81
00:02:28.200 --> 00:02:31.160
three, one, three, four, two,

82
00:02:31.160 --> 00:02:32.460
and five, adding up to

83
00:02:32.460 --> 00:02:34.780
twenty. Seven edges connect eight

84
00:02:34.780 --> 00:02:37.100
vertices with no cycle. Different

85
00:02:36.184 --> 00:02:38.640
tie choices may give different

86
00:02:38.640 --> 00:02:40.020
trees with the same minimum

87
00:02:40.020 --> 00:02:40.520
cost.

88
00:02:40.980 --> 00:02:42.560
Why is the greedy choice

89
00:02:42.560 --> 00:02:44.740
safe? Split the graph into

90
00:02:44.740 --> 00:02:46.380
the green tree and everything

91
00:02:46.380 --> 00:02:49.380
outside. The cheapest edge across

92
00:02:49.380 --> 00:02:51.020
that boundary can extend our

93
00:02:51.020 --> 00:02:52.640
tree towards a minimum spanning

94
00:02:52.640 --> 00:02:54.600
tree; this is the cut

95
00:02:54.600 --> 00:02:55.060
property.

96
00:02:55.420 --> 00:02:57.920
In code, each outside vertex

97
00:02:57.920 --> 00:02:59.920
remembers its cheapest edge into

98
00:02:59.920 --> 00:03:01.440
the tree and who offered

99
00:03:01.440 --> 00:03:03.480
it. For example, after we

100
00:03:03.480 --> 00:03:04.840
added C, E could join

101
00:03:04.840 --> 00:03:06.440
through C for one instead

102
00:03:06.440 --> 00:03:07.820
of through B for three.

103
00:03:08.360 --> 00:03:09.960
These are single edge costs,

104
00:03:10.340 --> 00:03:11.680
not full route distances.

105
00:03:12.500 --> 00:03:14.040
Now let us derive the

106
00:03:14.040 --> 00:03:16.557
running time instead of memorising

107
00:03:15.880 --> 00:03:18.040
a formula. Write n for

108
00:03:18.040 --> 00:03:19.400
the number of vertices and

109
00:03:19.400 --> 00:03:20.860
m for the number of

110
00:03:20.860 --> 00:03:23.480
undirected edges. Our graph has

111
00:03:23.480 --> 00:03:25.520
eight vertices and fourteen edges;

112
00:03:26.700 --> 00:03:28.340
runtime counts operations as these

113
00:03:28.340 --> 00:03:29.140
sizes grow.

114
00:03:29.740 --> 00:03:32.520
Two choices affect different jobs.

115
00:03:33.140 --> 00:03:34.820
An adjacency list or matrix

116
00:03:34.820 --> 00:03:36.000
tells us how to find

117
00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:38.300
a vertex's neighbours. An array

118
00:03:38.300 --> 00:03:40.220
or priority queue tells us

119
00:03:40.220 --> 00:03:41.420
how to select the cheapest

120
00:03:41.420 --> 00:03:44.060
outside vertex; an adjacency list

121
00:03:44.060 --> 00:03:45.620
and a binary heap are

122
00:03:45.620 --> 00:03:46.800
commonly used together.

123
00:03:47.220 --> 00:03:49.180
Consider the eager version, with

124
00:03:49.180 --> 00:03:50.800
one key per vertex. It

125
00:03:50.800 --> 00:03:52.140
removes each of the n

126
00:03:52.140 --> 00:03:54.120
vertices once, including the starting

127
00:03:54.120 --> 00:03:56.420
vertex with key zero. As

128
00:03:56.420 --> 00:03:58.000
each vertex enters the tree,

129
00:03:58.440 --> 00:03:59.913
it inspects that vertex's neighbours

130
00:04:00.320 --> 00:04:01.760
and improves keys when a

131
00:04:01.760 --> 00:04:03.360
cheaper joining edge is found.

132
00:04:03.740 --> 00:04:05.920
With an unsorted array, inspect

133
00:04:05.920 --> 00:04:07.440
every remaining key to find

134
00:04:07.440 --> 00:04:09.220
the smallest. Here the scans

135
00:04:09.220 --> 00:04:11.260
inspect eight entries, then seven,

136
00:04:11.260 --> 00:04:12.620
down to one, which makes

137
00:04:12.925 --> 00:04:15.160
thirty-six key inspections. In general,

138
00:04:15.580 --> 00:04:16.820
n plus n minus one

139
00:04:16.820 --> 00:04:18.100
down to one equals n

140
00:04:18.100 --> 00:04:19.380
times n plus one over

141
00:04:19.380 --> 00:04:21.780
two, so all selections together

142
00:04:21.780 --> 00:04:23.240
cost order n squared.

143
00:04:23.780 --> 00:04:26.600
With adjacency lists, A lists

144
00:04:26.600 --> 00:04:28.400
just B, C, and D.

145
00:04:28.400 --> 00:04:30.340
Over the whole run, every

146
00:04:30.340 --> 00:04:32.160
vertex's list is scanned once;

147
00:04:32.640 --> 00:04:34.460
each undirected edge appears at

148
00:04:34.460 --> 00:04:36.057
both endpoints. There are twenty-eight

149
00:04:36.057 --> 00:04:38.680
neighbour entries here, or twice

150
00:04:38.680 --> 00:04:40.540
m in general, so the

151
00:04:40.540 --> 00:04:42.320
total neighbour checking costs order

152
00:04:42.320 --> 00:04:44.080
n plus m, not n

153
00:04:44.080 --> 00:04:44.780
times m.

154
00:04:45.260 --> 00:04:47.640
Combine the two independent costs:

155
00:04:48.260 --> 00:04:50.080
quadratic time to select vertices,

156
00:04:50.580 --> 00:04:52.760
plus linear graph scanning. That

157
00:04:52.760 --> 00:04:54.340
gives order n squared plus

158
00:04:54.340 --> 00:04:56.060
m with adjacency lists and

159
00:04:56.060 --> 00:04:58.120
an unsorted key array. A

160
00:04:58.120 --> 00:04:59.900
list saves neighbour work, but

161
00:04:59.900 --> 00:05:01.100
it does not remove the

162
00:05:01.100 --> 00:05:02.280
repeated minimum scans.

163
00:05:02.900 --> 00:05:04.760
A matrix stores a possible

164
00:05:04.760 --> 00:05:06.380
connection for every pair of

165
00:05:06.380 --> 00:05:08.340
vertices, even where no edge

166
00:05:08.340 --> 00:05:10.700
exists. Scan eight cells in

167
00:05:10.700 --> 00:05:12.040
each of eight rows here;

168
00:05:12.660 --> 00:05:14.660
in general, n rows times

169
00:05:14.660 --> 00:05:16.580
n cells costs order n

170
00:05:16.580 --> 00:05:18.800
squared. With array selection, add

171
00:05:18.800 --> 00:05:20.420
the two quadratic costs to

172
00:05:20.420 --> 00:05:22.040
get order n squared, not

173
00:05:22.040 --> 00:05:22.960
n to the fourth.

174
00:05:23.260 --> 00:05:25.460
A binary heap roughly doubles

175
00:05:25.460 --> 00:05:27.040
its capacity at each new

176
00:05:27.040 --> 00:05:28.780
level, so its height grows

177
00:05:28.780 --> 00:05:30.380
like log n. Removing the

178
00:05:30.380 --> 00:05:32.080
minimum can sift down, while

179
00:05:32.080 --> 00:05:33.360
lowering a key can sift

180
00:05:33.360 --> 00:05:35.120
up, crossing at most that

181
00:05:35.120 --> 00:05:37.120
many levels. Keep an index

182
00:05:37.120 --> 00:05:38.380
from each vertex to its

183
00:05:38.380 --> 00:05:40.480
heap position, so locating a

184
00:05:40.480 --> 00:05:41.800
key does not require another

185
00:05:41.800 --> 00:05:42.500
linear search.

186
00:05:42.980 --> 00:05:45.000
Now multiply counts by heap

187
00:05:45.000 --> 00:05:47.340
costs. There are n removals

188
00:05:47.340 --> 00:05:48.660
at order log n each.

189
00:05:49.240 --> 00:05:50.640
Each edge can improve an

190
00:05:50.640 --> 00:05:52.200
outside key only when its

191
00:05:52.200 --> 00:05:53.980
first endpoint enters; when the

192
00:05:53.980 --> 00:05:55.740
second enters, both endpoints are

193
00:05:55.740 --> 00:05:58.100
already inside. Therefore there are

194
00:05:58.100 --> 00:06:00.200
at most m decreases, each

195
00:06:00.200 --> 00:06:02.180
costing order log n. Our

196
00:06:02.180 --> 00:06:03.740
trace has eight removals and

197
00:06:03.740 --> 00:06:05.860
nine improvements; fourteen is an

198
00:06:05.860 --> 00:06:07.640
upper bound, not the observed

199
00:06:07.640 --> 00:06:08.400
update count.

200
00:06:08.780 --> 00:06:10.800
Initialising keys and building the

201
00:06:10.800 --> 00:06:12.780
heap costs order n. Add

202
00:06:12.780 --> 00:06:15.180
graph scanning, removals, and decreases

203
00:06:15.180 --> 00:06:16.500
to get order n plus

204
00:06:16.500 --> 00:06:18.640
m, times log n. For

205
00:06:18.640 --> 00:06:20.460
a connected graph, m is

206
00:06:20.460 --> 00:06:21.660
at least n minus one,

207
00:06:21.920 --> 00:06:23.480
so this is commonly simplified

208
00:06:23.480 --> 00:06:25.040
to order m log n.

209
00:06:25.460 --> 00:06:27.440
A heap speeds up selection;

210
00:06:28.020 --> 00:06:29.280
it does not change how

211
00:06:29.280 --> 00:06:31.440
a matrix finds neighbours. Keep

212
00:06:31.440 --> 00:06:33.060
the n squared scanning term,

213
00:06:33.460 --> 00:06:34.740
then add the heap term:

214
00:06:34.860 --> 00:06:36.460
the quantity n plus m,

215
00:06:37.060 --> 00:06:39.200
multiplied by log n. On

216
00:06:39.200 --> 00:06:40.660
a dense simple graph, a

217
00:06:40.660 --> 00:06:41.880
list with a binary heap

218
00:06:41.880 --> 00:06:43.380
has an n squared log

219
00:06:43.380 --> 00:06:45.180
n upper bound, while the

220
00:06:45.180 --> 00:06:46.960
matrix and array version remains

221
00:06:46.960 --> 00:06:47.420
quadratic.

222
00:06:48.060 --> 00:06:50.120
A Fibonacci heap changes the

223
00:06:50.120 --> 00:06:52.040
price of a decrease: constant

224
00:06:52.346 --> 00:06:54.280
amortised time instead of logarithmic

225
00:06:54.280 --> 00:06:56.760
time. Removals still cost log

226
00:06:56.760 --> 00:06:59.200
n amortised, so adjacency lists

227
00:06:59.200 --> 00:07:00.820
give m plus n log

228
00:07:00.820 --> 00:07:03.460
n time. Ordinary binary heaps

229
00:07:03.460 --> 00:07:04.680
do not have that cheaper

230
00:07:04.680 --> 00:07:05.700
decrease operation.

231
00:07:06.260 --> 00:07:08.520
For space, adjacency lists store

232
00:07:08.520 --> 00:07:10.440
order n plus m entries,

233
00:07:10.900 --> 00:07:12.400
while a matrix stores order

234
00:07:12.400 --> 00:07:14.320
n squared cells. Eager Prim

235
00:07:14.320 --> 00:07:16.800
adds order n keys, parent

236
00:07:16.800 --> 00:07:19.060
values, membership flags, and heap

237
00:07:19.060 --> 00:07:21.360
positions. A lazy edge-heap version

238
00:07:21.360 --> 00:07:22.980
is different: it can queue

239
00:07:22.980 --> 00:07:24.880
order m edge records and

240
00:07:24.880 --> 00:07:26.280
has an order m log

241
00:07:26.280 --> 00:07:28.340
m time bound with adjacency

242
00:07:28.340 --> 00:07:28.600
lists.

243
00:07:29.100 --> 00:07:30.540
Things to take note of.

244
00:07:31.100 --> 00:07:33.360
Use an undirected weighted graph;

245
00:07:33.800 --> 00:07:35.580
negative costs are allowed, and

246
00:07:35.580 --> 00:07:37.500
equal costs need a consistent

247
00:07:37.500 --> 00:07:39.380
tie rule. If the graph

248
00:07:39.380 --> 00:07:41.720
is disconnected, restart from another

249
00:07:41.720 --> 00:07:43.100
component to get a minimum

250
00:07:43.100 --> 00:07:44.000
spanning forest.

251
00:07:44.500 --> 00:07:46.840
Prim grows one tree. Look

252
00:07:46.840 --> 00:07:48.400
at every edge leaving that

253
00:07:48.400 --> 00:07:50.680
tree, choose the cheapest, and

254
00:07:50.680 --> 00:07:52.260
bring in one new vertex.

255
00:07:52.920 --> 00:07:54.740
Keep doing that until everybody

256
00:07:54.740 --> 00:07:56.880
is connected; there, much easier

257
00:07:56.880 --> 00:07:58.140
when you can actually see

258
00:07:58.140 --> 00:07:58.760
the boundary.
